    <section>
      <h2>Alice in Turinland</h2>

      <p>What better way to discover Torino than to see it through your own eyes, with the thrill of a game making you see things in a new light time and time again?</p>

      <Figure src="social/alice-in-turinland.png" alt="Alice in Turinland game illustration" />

      <p>That is exactly what <strong>Alice in Turinland</strong> offers: a <strong>one-of-a-kind urban adventure</strong> that transforms the historic streets of Augusta Taurinorum into your playground, where every corner hides a <strong>challenge</strong> and every landmark becomes a <strong>checkpoint in a city-wide race</strong> against rival teams. Picture yourself standing on top of the Monte dei Cappuccini with the sight of the whole city before you, savoring a rich cappuccino as you <strong>plot your next move</strong>, before diving back into the labyrinth of Torino’s squares and arcades to taste the authentic flavors of the region. This is a competition in which <strong>your team battles others for the highest score</strong>, completing tasks that test your creativity and teamwork, all while indulging in the <strong>cuisine of Northern Italy that fuels your quest</strong>.</p>

      <p>And just when you think you have mastered the game, the real twist awaits: our <strong>organizers will embody the Kings</strong> of Hearts, Diamonds, Clubs, and Spades. Depending on which sovereign you choose, you will face a <strong>completely personalized challenge</strong>. Will you remain loyal to your teammates? Will fortune favor you when the cards are dealt? Will you prove sharp enough to read the minds of the Kings? These tailor-made challenges are primarily designed to <strong>forge bonds</strong>. For those who rise to the occasion, incredible prizes await that will forever remind you of this - hopefully glorious - day. </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Night at the museum</h2>

      <p><em>Are you ready for a night of history, mystery in an intimate atmosphere?</em></p>

      <Figure src="social/museo-egizio-1.jpg" alt="The Egyptian Museum in Torino" layout="inset">
        <p>Well, for one night, you will have the opportunity to explore one of the world's richest collections of <strong>ancient Egyptian artefacts</strong> in an exclusive after-hours event. Home to over 40,000 findings, the <strong>Egyptian museum</strong> in Torino is considered the oldest museum in the world dedicated entirely to ancient Egyptian civilization and the most important outside of Egypt, preserving more than four millennia of history through a vast collection of archaeological items telling the story of one of humanity's greatest civilizations.</p>
      </Figure>

      <p>Hosted <strong>after the museum's regular opening hours</strong>, the event will feature a guided tour through the galleries. Be ready for an exciting night of adventure in quiet museum halls that will make you feel like you’re crossing millennia and continents… And release the <strong>Ben Stiller</strong> in you!</p>

      <p>The evening will be organized in collaboration with <strong>Club Silencio</strong>, a Torino-based cultural organization that transforms museums, historical buildings, and cultural venues into vibrant spaces where heritage, music and social life come together. As one of the main social events, it is designed to offer participants a meaningful way to experience Torino while naturally encouraging interaction and exchange in a relaxed setting. The atmosphere will be relaxed and informal, allowing participants to enjoy a calm and easy-going evening.</p>

      <Figure src="social/museo-egizio-2.jpg" alt="Galleries of the Egyptian Museum in Torino" />

      <p>Following the Club Silencio format, the event will combine the guided visit with <strong>live music</strong> and an Italian-style <strong>aperitivo</strong> served within the museum, creating a relaxed atmosphere that supports conversation and networking.</p>

      <p>Beyond its cultural value, the evening will provide a space for participants from all over the world to meet outside the conference program, sharing an informal moment in one of Torino's most iconic museums. </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Closing ceremony</h2>

      <p>The closing ceremony will take place at <strong>3:00 P.M.</strong> on <strong>Friday</strong>, 8th August. Following the traditional <strong>commemorative group photo</strong>, a hallmark of every IAPS event, all participants will gather inside <strong>Campus Luigi Einaudi</strong>, where the final chapter of the conference will begin.</p>

      <p>The ceremony will open with the announcement of the <strong>winners of the various games</strong> and activities held throughout the week, who will be awarded their well-deserved prizes. Afterwards, the <strong>General Coordinators</strong> will officially close the conference, thanking all participants, volunteers and organizers for contributing to an unforgettable week.</p>

      <p>As the event closes, we hope that participants will carry with them the happiness of having shared meaningful experiences. Yet the conference is not over just yet: <strong>two final activities</strong> will provide the definitive farewell, bringing the week to a memorable conclusion worthy of an event that celebrates both scientific curiosity and the spirit of the IAPS community.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Torino Magica</h2>

      <Figure src="social/torino-notturna.jpg" alt="Torino by night" />

      <p>We believe that science, and physics along with it, were born from humanity's desire to understand the world around us. Long before the scientific method, myths and legends represented humanity's first attempts to explain natural phenomena through curious observation and imagination. Ultimately, it is from this continuous search for answers that knowledge itself emerges. For this reason, we have designed an activity that brings together the <strong>rigor of science</strong> and the <strong>charm of traditional folklore</strong>, showing how both stem from the same inexhaustible human curiosity.</p>

      <p>Italy enjoys a vast cultural heritage across many fields, including science, art, history and gastronomy, but also a wealth of <strong>legends and myths</strong> passed down through the centuries. Follow us and we will take you on a journey to discover them. Through this event, we would like to introduce participants to a lesser-known side of our cultural heritage by exploring the city through its legends and symbolism, leaving everyone free to draw their own interpretations.</p>

      <Figure src="social/gran-madre.jpg" alt="Gran Madre church in Torino" layout="inset" imageFirst={false}>
        <p>We therefore propose a guided evening tour through <strong>Torino Magica</strong>, a city that beautifully combines historical heritage and mystery. Torino is widely known as the first capital of Italy, for its automotive heritage, its chocolate, cinema, jazz, and historic cafés. It is also famous for its rich collection of <strong>local legends</strong>, which portray the city as a <strong>meeting point between opposing forces</strong>: light and darkness, reason and mystery.</p>
      </Figure>

      <p>These stories intertwine with the city's history and with the architectural choices made by the House of Savoy, creating a cultural heritage that is both fascinating and thought-provoking.</p>

      <p>According to one of Torino's most enduring legends, the city occupies a <strong>unique position</strong> within two mythical triangles that span the whole world. Whether regarded as folklore or simply as a captivating narrative, this duality has become an integral part of the city's cultural imagination. Throughout the centuries, monuments, churches, squares, and architectural details have been interpreted through the lens of these opposing traditions, giving rise to stories in which reason and mystery coexist.</p>

      <p>Following this legendary path, our tour will guide participants through the <strong>landmarks</strong> that have given life to these centuries-old narratives. Moving from the alleged center of reason to that of mystery, participants will discover how this symbolism can create a unique identity of the city. This experience aims to <strong>spark curiosity</strong> and demonstrate how history, art, symbolism, and folklore can <strong>coexist</strong> alongside rational thought. After all, every great scientific discovery begins with a question.</p>

      <p>At the end of the tour, only one final question will remain:</p>

      <p><em>Which side of Torino Magica will you choose to believe in?</em></p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Final night</h2>

      <p><em>Leap into the History, Dive into the Light.</em></p>

      <p>The final night of ICPS will take you to one of Torino’s most precious gems: <strong>Villa della Regina</strong>, the Queen's Villa. Atop the hills, overlooking the city, this royal residence was for centuries the favored retreat of princesses and queens of the <strong>House of Savoy</strong>.</p>

      <p>Built in the early 17th century as a countryside vineyard estate (or “<em>vigna</em>”) for Prince Cardinal Maurizio of Savoy, the villa was later transformed into a lavish <strong>Baroque residence</strong>. It was here that the Savoy sovereigns would spend their time, surrounded by Italian-style gardens. On the final night of ICPS 2028 this UNESCO World Heritage site will host our celebration, offering a location in which to <strong>party like no other</strong>.</p>

      <h3>The Light and Glow night</h3>

      <Figure src="social/villa-regina.jpg" alt="Villa della Regina in Torino" />

      <p>The intriguing history of the villa will soon give way to the party. Because the "Light and Glow night" is meant to be a true <strong>Italian-style party</strong> in the most authentic sense of the word. Picture this: at the entrance, every participant will receive a <strong>fluorescent gadget</strong>, ready to light up the night. The evening will kick off with an explosion of music and light shows, creating a fascinating contrast between Baroque elegance and contemporary energy. </p>

      <p>This, though, is only the beginning; the magical moment has yet to begin. The <strong>lights will go out</strong>, and the party will take on a new hue: welcome to the "glow" phase. The villa will turn into a vibrant <strong>neon glow party</strong>, where your gadgets will come to life, enhanced by special effects and a haze machine that will wrap the atmosphere in a shroud of mystery.</p>

      <p>And for a finale crowning the night, the sky over Torino will ignite with a <strong>fireworks display</strong>, a tribute of light that will mark the peak of our celebration.</p>

      <p>Get ready for a night where history meets the future, and light becomes the party.</p>

      <p><em>See you at Light and Glow night!</em></p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Museum visits</h2>

      <p>We strongly suggest to take some time to enjoy the <strong>cultural and artistic heritage</strong> of Northern Italy. Both Milano and Torino offer world-class cultural institutions that perfectly bridge the gap between humanistic beauty and scientific curiosity. Between one visit and the other, you can relax and socialize in vibrant urban spaces, beautiful parks, and breathtaking viewpoints, experiencing the authentic Italian lifestyle.</p>

      <h3>Milano: the cradle of scientific renaissance and urban culture</h3>

      <Figure src="social/duomo-milano.jpg" alt="The Duomo of Milano" />

      <p>Discover Milano’s unique combination of historical grandeur and technological edge by selecting one of the following venues:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>National museum of science and technology “Leonardo da Vinci”</strong>: As Italy's largest science and technology museum, this venue is a must-visit for physics students. Beyond the famous galleries dedicated to <strong>Leonardo’s mechanical drawings and physical experiments</strong>, the museum hosts a state-of-the-art permanent <strong>exhibition on Particle Physics</strong> developed in partnership with CERN, alongside a massive <strong>Space Pavilion</strong> featuring the only Moon rock fragment in Italy.</li>
        <li><strong>Civic planetarium “Ulrico Hoepli” &amp; Brera's botanical garden</strong>: Nestled in the historic <strong>Porta Venezia park</strong>, Italy’s largest planetarium offers <strong>immersive astrophysics shows</strong> under its majestic 19.6-meter dome. This can be coupled with a walking tour of the Brera district, home to the historic INAF Astronomical Observatory, built by Ruggero Boscovich, where Giovanni Schiaparelli mapped the canals of Mars.</li>
        <li><strong>Museum of natural history</strong>: The oldest civic museum in Milano, founded in <strong>1838</strong>. Housed in a stunning <strong>Neo-Gothic building</strong> (constructed 1892&nbsp;1907), its first-floor exhibitions feature spectacular, historic dioramas illustrating the world’s major natural ecosystems. This exhibition layout is unique in Italy for its scale and historical value.</li>
        <li><strong>Duomo di Milano &amp; historical terraces</strong>: For those seeking a classic cultural experience, architectural tours will take students to the <strong>rooftop terraces</strong> of Milano's magnificent <strong>Gothic cathedral</strong>, offering a breathtaking view of the city skyline stretching all the way to the Alps.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Torino: royal elegance and the gateway to the Alps</h3>

      <Figure src="social/museo-torino.jpg" alt="Museo Torino" />

      <p>In the second half of the event, participants will transition to Torino, Italy's first capital, renowned for its baroque architecture, deep scientific roots, industrial pioneering, and stunning alpine backdrop. Participants who stay slightly longer after the conference’s closure will be invited to visit one of the following:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>National cinema museum</strong>: Located inside Torino's architectural symbol, the <strong>Mole Antonelliana</strong>, this museum is a spectacular journey through the history of moving images. Physics students will appreciate the interactive sections dedicated to pre-cinema optical toys, shadows, stereoscopy, and the physics of light refraction.</li>
        <li><strong>Car museum (MAUTO)</strong>: Reflecting Torino’s status as an industrial powerhouse, MAUTO showcases the <strong>evolution of the automobile</strong>. The scientific focus here covers car design, vehicle dynamics, aerodynamics, and the physics of thermodynamic efficiency and lightweight materials.</li>
        <li><strong>Royal Palace of Torino (Musei Reali) &amp; Royal Gardens</strong>: A spectacular journey through the history of the <strong>Savoy dynasty</strong>. The visit includes the lavish royal apartments, the Royal Armoury, and the majestic Chapel of the Holy Shroud.</li>
        <li><strong>Choco-story Torino (Museum of Chocolate and Gianduja)</strong>: Torino is the Italian capital of <strong>chocolate</strong> (the birthplace of <em>Gianduja</em>). This interactive museum offers a fun, sweet, and culturally enriching experience for attendees wanting a taste of local traditions.</li>
      </ul>
